<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kana">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hiragana, katakana, man'yōgana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kana are syllabaries used to write the morae of Japanese: today hiragana and katakana, and historically also man'yōgana, Chinese characters used for their sounds, and hentaigana, older variants of hiragana. The name means borrowed name, in contrast to kanji used for their meaning. Each kana stands for a vowel, a consonant and vowel, or the final nasal n, so the system represents spoken Japanese closely. Phonetic kanji were once called men's kana, while hiragana, derived from cursive man'yōgana, was called women's script. Tradition credits the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a> with inventing kana in the ninth century, and katakana are also used to write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kana.jpg" alt="Kana" width="330" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara no Teika (1162-1241) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tosa_nikki_copied_by_Teika.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
